summary:
The CPU copy of a 1531 or 1532 map is the map object in the shared segment. It holds two bytes_vec buffers, both sized by the server when the map is created (nv_gpu_model_maps.cpp:75-95; map_handler.cpp:1622-1640): `data_buffer` ([key][value], the summed words) and `status_buffer` (8 u64 status words). A CPU update returns EPERM and a delete EINVAL. get_next_key is index arithmetic (cpp:114-132).

Registration happens in the agent, at the first `get_gpu_mem_buffer` (cpp:683-775). That call is reached through create_map_basic_info -> get_gpu_map_extra_buffer (bpf_attach_ctx_cuda.cpp:531,553-554; map_handler.cpp:1921-1941, which also pins one refcount per process). In order:
- it claims OWNER_PID by CAS in shm (cpp:703-721);
- it sets shards = next_pow2(SMs) x 32 (cpp:722-737);
- it allocates with cuMemAlloc and zeroes on its own stream (cuMemsetD8Async), then calls cuStreamSynchronize under agent_sync_scope, in relaxed capture mode (cpp:738-767);
- it calls register_device_array (cpp:610-663). That sets the schedule (sum maps: busy 20 ms, idle 200 ms, sync gap 2 ms; per-thread maps: 1000/2000/1000, all from env), allocates pinned staging of entries x W x 8 bytes (cuMemAllocHost + cuMemHostGetDevicePointer, cpp:633-641), and for the first map records the owner and context, sets the sync listener and atexit, and starts drain_loop (cpp:642-662).

The snapshot is one PTX module (`.version 7.0 .target sm_70`, cpp:144-197). Its single entry, `bpftime_model_snapshot(src, dst, n, w, s)`, runs grid-stride over the output words. It sums S shards with `ld.relaxed.gpu.global` on the cvta.to.global address (the form of the programs' red/atom .global adds) and writes `st.u64` into pinned staging. prepare_locked loads it once, with one CUfunction and one warm-up launch, on a non-blocking stream (cpp:311-342).

deliver_locked (cpp:358-466):
- copies the map list and reads app_sync_count before enqueueing (cpp:377; the ordering argument is at cpp:350-357);
- launches at most 1024 x 256 threads per due map (cpp:396-403) and waits by polling cuStreamQuery (cpp:294-308);
- copies staging into host_words with relaxed atomic stores, only for words that changed, in ascending order (cpp:420-428);
- stores SYNCS_COVERED and EXIT_DELIVERY relaxed, then does a release fetch_add on DELIVERIES (cpp:431-440), then updates the schedule (cpp:444-455);
- on any set-up or snapshot failure, sets `s.broken` for all maps (cpp:334-339, 414-419).

Three callers run deliveries:
- the drain thread (cpp:487-527), woken by the schedule or by on_app_sync (cpp:278-289);
- at-sync delivery (BPFTIME_GPU_DELIVERY_AT_SYNC=1), inside the application's synchronize call, only for maps whose syncs are not yet covered (cpp:383-389, 592-605);
- the atexit handler (cpp:535-588). It writes SYNCS_AT_EXIT and LAUNCHES_AFTER_LAST_SYNC, stops the thread (waits 500 ms), try-locks run_mutex (200 ms) and delivers every map with a 1 s deadline.

CPU readers: elem_lookup acquire-loads DELIVERIES (cpp:101-102). It then returns a pointer into data_buffer, or status word k-max_entries for k in max_entries..+7 (cpp:103-106). The syscall server memcpys value_size bytes after the call returns (syscall_context.cpp:962-970). So the reader's plain copy may overlap the next delivery's per-word relaxed stores. There is no seqlock: every word is an independent monotonic counter.

No unit test covers delivery. Greps for deliver_now, SYNCS_COVERED and BPFTIME_GPU_DELIVERY outside the sources found nothing; runtime/CMakeLists.txt:110 only compiles the file. attach/nv_attach_impl/test/test_sum_map_routing.cpp covers the routing pass only.

touch_points.for_1533:
A second entry in the same module, e.g. bpftime_model_hash_snapshot(dir, rows, ctrl, dst, slots, R, S, W). Per slot:
- ld.relaxed.gpu.global of the header on the cvta.to.global address;
- if live: fence.acq_rel.gpu, which forms the acquire pattern of PTX 8.8 with the inserter's st.release. fence is PTX ISA 6.0, so .version 7.0 / sm_70 suffice; do not use fence.acquire, which needs PTX 8.6 (local ptx-isa.txt:26411,26421);
- then sum the row's S shards (row < R) or read its single copy (row >= R) with ld.relaxed.gpu.global;
- write the raw header and the sums to staging. Non-live headers keep claiming and refused distinct from empty.
Also sum the per-SM loss counters and read the claim count with relaxed loads. Needs a second CUfunction and its own warm-up launch.
